---
// About: what NepDate is, where its data comes from and who stands behind it. Agents and readers
// check this page before recommending the project.
import InfoPage from "../components/InfoPage.astro";
import { HUB, MITIBAR_REPO, PATRO, ports } from "../data/ports";

const languages = new Intl.ListFormat("en", { type: "conjunction" }).format(ports.map((port) => port.name));
const description =
  "About NepDate: open source Bikram Sambat (Nepali calendar) libraries for every major language, a free online patro and the MitiBar desktop calendar, built from one dataset and one specification.";
const site = Astro.site?.href ?? "https://nepdate.com/";
const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "AboutPage",
  name: "About NepDate",
  url: new URL("/about/", site).href,
  description,
  about: { "@id": `${site}#org` },
};
---

<InfoPage title="About NepDate" heading="About NepDate" description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <p class="info-lede">
    NepDate is a family of open source libraries and apps for the Bikram Sambat (BS) calendar, the official calendar of
    Nepal. It answers the same question the same way in every language: which day is it, in BS and in AD.
  </p>

  <h2>What it is</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Libraries for {ports.length} languages:</strong>{" "}
      {languages}. Each converts between BS and AD for every day from BS 1901 to 2199, formats and parses dates in
      Nepali and English, and works with months, ranges and fiscal years. A separate patro package adds tithi, public
      holidays and events. See the <a href="/docs/">documentation</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A free online patro</strong> at <a href={PATRO}>patro.nepdate.com</a>, with a page for every month and
      day.
    </li>
    <li><strong>MitiBar</strong>, a <a href="/mitibar/">desktop calendar</a> for Windows, macOS and Linux.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where it comes from</h2>
  <p>
    NepDate started from the C# <a href="https://github.com/RajuPrasai/NepDate">NepDate</a> library, which remains the
    reference: every other language is tested against answers recorded from it. The calendar data and the
    specification live once, in the <a href={HUB}>hub repository</a>, and every language builds from that copy. Where
    the month lengths, tithi and holidays come from is written down in{" "}
    <a href="/docs/project/data/">Data and credits</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is run</h2>
  <p>
    NepDate is maintained in the open on GitHub by its contributors. All code is MIT licensed, every change goes
    through a public pull request with tests in each language, and releases are published to each language's own
    registry from those repositories. There is no company, paid tier or advertising behind it. The{" "}
    <a href={MITIBAR_REPO}>MitiBar source</a> is public too.
  </p>

  <h2>What it is not</h2>
  <p>
    NepDate is not affiliated with the Government of Nepal. Holiday and tithi data is informational: for an official
    notice, check the government's own publication. To report a wrong date, or to reach the project, see{" "}
    <a href="/contact/">Contact</a>.
  </p>
</InfoPage>
